fuse_file_info::stream and ::nonseekable
========================================

Both are set in open() or create(). With either, lseek(), pread() and
pwrite() on the file fail with ESPIPE.


When to use stream
------------------

For files that behave like a pipe or socket:

  - The data is produced, not stored (event feed, command/reply channel,
    device). Reading the same offset twice would not return the same
    bytes.
  - A read may wait for data that a write on the same fd produces.
  - The file is opened with direct_io. This is required.
  - The filesystem does not need an offset, it always gets 0.


When to use nonseekable
-----------------------

For files with fixed contents that can only be read in order (generated
report, decompressed stream, backend that cannot seek):

  - The file uses the page cache or passthrough.
  - The filesystem wants the offset, it advances with each read.
  - Reads and writes on one fd never wait for each other.


What goes wrong with the wrong choice
-------------------------------------

| Choice                        | Result                                  |
|-------------------------------|-----------------------------------------|
| nonseekable, socket-like file | read waiting for write on same fd hangs |
| stream without direct_io      | end of file, or first bytes repeated    |
| stream, size 0, io_uring/AIO  | every read returns end of file          |


Why
---

nonseekable keeps a file position. read() and write() hold the file
position lock (f_pos_lock) of the open file while the request runs, if
the process has threads or the fd is shared through fork(), dup() or fd
passing. A write then waits for a read on the same open file to finish,
and only one read or write on it runs at a time.

stream has no file position and takes no lock. read() and write() pass
offset 0. With direct_io the filesystem picks the data, so offset 0 does
no harm. The page cache and passthrough use the offset themselves and
always access offset 0. io_uring and AIO reads with direct_io return end
of file without asking the filesystem when the offset is at or past the
file size.

splice() and sendfile() use and advance the file position even for
stream, and io_uring passes an explicit offset through for both.


Kernel support
--------------

FOPEN_STREAM exists since Linux 5.2 and was backported to stable kernels
down to 3.16.70. Older kernels ignore it and apply FOPEN_NONSEEKABLE,
which libfuse sends along with it. Directories behave as nonseekable
with both flags.
